Reading.  If the floor's ensemble between the two grid points were a mixture of the two states, every mean would be linear in the weight: the sixth column would be 1 at every point
and the two lever-rule columns equal.  The occupancy is a mean of 1000 exact samples (standard error about 0.15 sites near the transition, i.e. about 0.02 in the weight); the slope is exact.
The displaced state is not one state: between its first pure grid point and the largest lambda its slope still grows (see the tables of the four logs), which limits the agreement.
The control: with lambda as the abscissa (a smooth response between the same two grid points) the same points are off by the factors of the seventh column.
